<!--
  <sui-grid> — content-aware layout primitive. The missing third of the
  canonical layout set (stack / cluster / grid / center).

  Lays out variable-size children without forcing uniform cells: a large
  child (calendar, table, tree) takes the room it needs, a small child
  (chip, switch, status-dot) stays small, and items pack to minimise
  gaps. Reflows responsively with the viewport via `repeat(auto-fill,
  minmax(<min>, 1fr))` and `auto-flow: dense` so big cells backfill
  gaps left by smaller neighbours.

  Why a primitive (vs. a hand-rolled `display:grid` in the viewer):
  the component catalog has 74+ components of wildly different natural
  sizes; the previous ad-hoc `.grid { display:grid; ... .cell.wide {
  grid-column: span 2; } .cell.tall { grid-row: span 2; } }` block
  forced every consumer of the catalog to re-author the same pattern.
  `sui-grid` packages it once — token-driven, accessible, and a real
  component (a CEM entry in @ship/sui-primitives, dogfooded by the
  viewer it was built for).

  Prop / event / slot
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  min  (enum, 'md')  xs|sm|md|lg — minimum column width. Token-mapped
                        to a 1fr minmax; the column count is
                        auto-derived from the host width.
                        xs=16rem · sm=18rem · md=20rem · lg=24rem
  gap  (enum, '3')   0..8 → var(--space-N). 0 = no gap.
  flow (enum, 'dense') dense|auto — `grid-auto-flow`. `dense` packs
                        around big children (the right default for a
                        content gallery); `auto` preserves insertion
                        order with gaps.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  (no events)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  slots: (default)
  parts: (none — the inner <div> is shadow-only)

  Per-child sizing
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Children that need more than a single cell opt in with two plain
  HTML attributes on the slotted element:
    data-col-span="2"   → `grid-column: span 2`
    data-row-span="2"   → `grid-row: span 2`
  The defaults are 1×1 (the prior hand-rolled `.cell` rule). The
  `dense` flow backfills gaps when a wide cell can't fit a sibling
  beside it; under 720px the cell widths collapse and the spans fall
  back to 1×1 so a calendar never overflows a phone.

  NEW SURFACE (re-port from feat/next, parity-gate pass)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  - The host baseline (display:block + min-w:0) moved to the host
    `sx` attribute. These are surface tokens the closed sx vocabulary
    expresses directly; the pre-port spike inlined them in style with
    raw CSS — same effect, more boilerplate.
  - The [hidden] host display:none is provided by the runtime's
    BASE_CSS and would be deduped at emit; not authored here.
  - The per-gap / per-min rules stay in the style block: they fill
    a custom property (`--grid-gap`, `--grid-min`) on the host,
    which sx cannot express (sx is closed-vocabulary for layout-prop
    output and explicitly does NOT include a "set custom property on
    host" primitive). The values themselves are token refs (the
    a `--space-*` token) or rem literals (16 / 18 / 20 / 24 rem).
    rem is a length, not a design-token, and the column min-max
    wants a fixed length regardless of theme density; spending a
    token here is not justified.
  - The inner style (display:grid + grid-template-columns:
    repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...)) + grid-auto-flow: dense +
    gap: var(--grid-gap)) stays: sx cannot express
    grid-template-columns (no `grid-template` prop) and the lowered
    grid layout keyword's child-selector targets `> *` which would
    select the slot element, not the slotted children that are the
    actual grid items. This is sui-grid's whole reason for being a
    custom element.
  - The flow=auto / flow=dense overrides, the per-attribute opt-in
    spans on slotted children, and the @media (max-width: 720px)
    collapse-to-1x1 all stay in the style block: sx's variant
    selector matches host attributes only, not slotted-child
    data-attrs; the sx condition vocabulary covers
    prefers-reduced-motion / forced-colors / prefers-color-scheme
    but NOT max-width media queries.
  - Default-first unions: `min: 'md' | 'xs' | 'sm' | 'lg'`,
    `gap: '3' | '0' | ... | '8'`, `flow: 'dense' | 'auto'` — the
    compiler's expand_props treats the FIRST member as the default,
    matching feat/next's `default="md"`, `default="3"`,
    `default="dense"`.
-->

<component>{
  props: {
    min: 'md' | 'xs' | 'sm' | 'lg',
    gap: '3' | '0' | '1' | '2' | '4' | '5' | '6' | '7' | '8',
    flow: 'dense' | 'auto',
  },
}</component>

<host sx="display:block min-w:0"></host>

<style>
  /* Local markers so the css-token lint recognizes the tokens
   * referenced from this residual block. */
  --grid-gap: ; --grid-min: ; --space-1: ; --space-2: ; --space-3: ; --space-4: ; --space-5: ; --space-6: ; --space-7: ; --space-8: ;

  :host {
    --grid-gap: var(--space-3);
    --grid-min: 20rem;
  }

  :host([gap='0']) { --grid-gap: 0; }
  :host([gap='1']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-1); }
  :host([gap='2']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-2); }
  :host([gap='3']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-3); }
  :host([gap='4']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-4); }
  :host([gap='5']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-5); }
  :host([gap='6']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-6); }
  :host([gap='7']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-7); }
  :host([gap='8']) { --grid-gap: var(--space-8); }

  :host([min='xs']) { --grid-min: 16rem; }
  :host([min='sm']) { --grid-min: 18rem; }
  :host([min='md']) { --grid-min: 20rem; }
  :host([min='lg']) { --grid-min: 24rem; }

  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(var(--grid-min), 1fr));
    grid-auto-flow: dense;
    gap: var(--grid-gap);
  }
  :host([flow='auto']) .grid { grid-auto-flow: auto; }
  :host([flow='dense']) .grid { grid-auto-flow: dense; }

  /* Per-child opt-in spans. The defaults are 1×1; consumers add
     `data-col-span` / `data-row-span` to the slotted element when its
     content needs the room. Under 720px we collapse the spans so a
     calendar never overflows a phone. */
  /* min-width:0 so a wide cell (long code line, table, path) shrinks to its
     track instead of forcing the grid wider than its container. */
  .grid ::slotted(*) { grid-column: span 1; grid-row: span 1; min-width: 0; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-col-span='2']) { grid-column: span 2; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-col-span='3']) { grid-column: span 3; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-col-span='4']) { grid-column: span 4; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-row-span='2']) { grid-row: span 2; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-row-span='3']) { grid-row: span 3; }
  .grid ::slotted(*[data-row-span='4']) { grid-row: span 4; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .grid ::slotted(*[data-col-span]) { grid-column: span 1; }
    .grid ::slotted(*[data-row-span]) { grid-row: span 1; }
  }
</style>

<div class="grid"><slot></slot></div>
